018 farm bill  but then  after that point anything that is  processed or manufactured  for its cannabinoid content so any of  those products that you've you know  probably been seeing around tinctures  food beverages  smokable hemp flour that is all  regulated uh by the office of cannabis  management um so the the program  originally started at the department of  health um and then similar to the  medical cannabis program was  transitioned over to this new office of  cannabis management when the  marijuana regulation and taxation act  the mrta  passed in uh march 31st of 2021  so  that transition happened in law uh  immediately but then it took several  months for us to actually stand up the  office and officially move people over  here hire new staff get a board  establish all those things so i think  officially uh it was the november um  third board meeting where we transferred  the regulations uh over to the office of  cannabis management which i guess we can  technically say you know the canada hemp  program um started uh here at the office  of cannabis management  and when we talk about cannabinoid hemp  and what we regulate  we really only only are talking about  products that are sold for their  cannabinoid content so not talking about  seed or  fiber products anything uh  related to just standard uh hemp food or  stuff like that that does not fall under  our regulatory jurisdiction um that  would still be under either ag markets  from a food perspective or the  department of health if you're like a  restaurant selling those those products  um and uh we were conscious to use the  term cannabinoid and not just cbd  um understanding there's many  cannabinoids in this plant and  uh as i i'm sure you've seen um you know  people are interested in in cannabinoids  beyond cbd and you know i think we're  starting to see you know cbn products  cbg products uh creep into the market  along with just general you know full  spectrum products that incorporate minor  cannabinoids terpenes and stuff like  that uh into their into their product  so the the reason for why this program  exists is really a stop gap measure uh  to to account for the fact that we have  uh hemp products cannabinoid hemp  products  widely available online  in stores throughout new york state and  throughout the country  however there's no federal regulations  that is that is governing  how these products should be  manufactured what should be included on  the label  um  because uh the fda has a primary  regulatory jurisdiction here they have  uh not taken any action really to to  regulate this industry because of a  pharmaceutically approved drug that has  cbd as an active ingredient in  um there's a number of bills in in  congress that would uh that would get fb  fda involved in the regulation of this  and kind of force their hand  i know they're looking at the issue i've  met with them i've talked with them  they're they're aware but you know  they're a very slow government agency  that is operating on on evidence that  typically takes years and years and  years to develop  and and research so um it's a bit of a  catch-22 at the moment and to to kind of  account for that fact that these  products are available um the  cannabinoid hemp program is intended to  offer some some guidance in terms of how  these products can be manufactured how  they can be labeled um and it's really  not meant to to reinvent the wheel  we're trying to point to regulations  that exist for similar products either  at the dietary supplement level or food  products or other cannabis products as  you see in like adult use or medical  cannabis programs  trying to kind of pull best practices  from from those three regulatory uh  you know kind of frameworks to to build  this this cannabinoid hemp program so  consumers are are aware of of what  they're getting um what's on the product  and uh you know i can't count the the  number of studies that have been done  out there where where a lab 